  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday Night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1104 AM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nOk! Now the good news. Relief from this heat is coming. On Monday, \nthe last official full day of summer, we'll maintain one more day of \nwell-above normal temperatures, but the ridge really starts to break \ndown. This means with the remaining heat, shower and storm chances \nwill be a little more widespread. I've taken a look at forecast \nsoundings on Monday and while not overly impressive, mid-level lapse \nrates of mid 6's and high triple digit DCAPE values could spell at \nleast a low wind risk. This would match up with the current Day 3 \nOutlook, though I would say that even though the current Marginal \nRisk is over the southeast half of the mid-state, any storms that \ndevelop will carry a wind risk.\n\nTuesday. Fall starts at 7:04 pm CT and it's also the day we've all \nbeen waiting for. Temperatures will begin backing off (low 90s/low \n80s Plateau for highs) and additional, likely even more widespread \nshowers and storms will become possible. This is as a frontal \nboundary approaches the mid-state. Forecast soundings are similar to \nMonday, but as we get into the evening, deep layer shear values \nincrease to about 30 kts. Now, I'm not talking about a severe \nweather outbreak or anything, but storms will be worth watching late \nafternoon into the early evening. Relief will be felt on Tuesday, \nthat's for sure, but starting Wednesday, behind the frontal passage, \ntemperatures really take a dive. That doesn't mean 60s and 70s for \neveryone (though the Plateau will see 70s), but the rest of Middle \nTN will see afternoon highs right around normal for this time of the \nyear (mid-80s). Super excited for that, coupled with what looks like \ndry conditions until late in the week.",
